\begin{slide}
Keeping Filters Awake
 \begin{itemize}
 \item Filter goes to sleep means error covariance for certain states becomes so small that measurements no longer have an effect
 \item Can be the result of round-off problems
 \item Presence of non-random, determinisitc quantities can be contributors
	\begin{itemize}
		\item Random constants are nearly deterministic
		\item Watch out for states not driven by process noise!
	\end{itemize}
\end{itemize}
\end{slide}

\begin{slide}
Keeping Filters Awake
\begin{itemize}
  \item Recall running average example
  \item That was a random constant
  \item Gain $1/N\rightarrow 0$ as $N\rightarrow\infty$
  \item CT I Filter has 12 states modeled as random constants, i.e., the non-varying states
\end{itemize} 
\end{slide}

